text:
Q: Getting the id/name of the state triggered by a boolean parameter in Unity animator controller A: <p>As far as I know, there's no way to get the states whose transitions have a specific condition type (e.g. trigger) in runtime. Conditions are exposed only in Editor side.</p>
<p>You can use <a href="https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.3/Documentation/ScriptReference/Animator.GetCurrentAnimatorStateInfo.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Animator.GetCurrentAnimatorStateInfo(int layerIndex)</a> to get the current state, provided you poll for changes. Given the layer index (likely 0 for you), you get An <code>AnimatorStateInfo</code> object back. Unfortunately the state does not provide the string name, but the string name's hash with <code>AnimatorStateInfo.shortNameHash</code> or <code>AnimatorStateInfo.fullPathHash</code>. The hash is generated using <a href="https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.3/Documentation/ScriptReference/Animator.StringToHash.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Animator.StringToHash</a>.</p>
<p>From the docs:</p>
<pre><code>The name should be in the form Layer.Name or Layer.SubStateMachine.Name. 
For example, Base.Idle or Base.RunSM.JogForward.
</code></pre>
<p>Short name hash is basically just &quot;JogForward&quot; and the full path is what is shown above.</p>
<p>At this point you have a couple of choices. You can either use <a href="https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.3/Documentation/ScriptReference/AnimatorStateInfo.IsName.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">AnimatorStateInfo.IsName(string name)</a> which calls the <code>Animator.StringToHash</code> internally like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var stateInfo = m_Animator.GetCurrentAnimatorStateInfo(0);
if (stateInfo.IsName(&quot;Base.Idle&quot;)) //providing a full path
{
   Debug.Log($&quot;Currently in state Base.Idle.&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
<p>or e.g. keep consts of hashes in your scripts and just checking against <code>state.shortNameHash</code>.</p>
<pre><code>const int RangedAttackHash = Animator.StringToHash(&quot;RangedAttack&quot;)

void Update()
{
  var stateInfo = m_Animator.GetCurrentAnimatorStateInfo(0);
  if(stateInfo.shortNameHash == RangedAttackHash)
  {
    ...
  }

  //or
  switch(stateInfo.shortNameHash)
  {
     case RangedAttackHash:...
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>That being said, there are some extra ways to achieve the effect you want. One is <a href="https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.3/Documentation/Manual/script-AnimationWindowEvent.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Animation Events</a>. You can add events in the animation clips which fire function calls from scripts when played, although it's not super robust (there are cases where events either don't fire or misfire). And another is to use <a href="https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.3/Documentation/ScriptReference/StateMachineBehaviour.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">StateMachineBehaviours</a>. You can write scripts that can run on StateEnter/StateExit etc.</p>
<p>Seems this is more appropriate for you. You can write this script once and it should work on any animator after that. You can get components through the <code>Animator.gameObject</code> inherited member, get your intended Component and call a function e.g. <code>BlockStuffWhileAnimatingAttack()</code> on StateEnter (and provided the current state hash is equal to the RangedAttack's) and revert those changes on StateExit.</p>
<p>You could probably just use tags. Tag any state which you intend to block stuff during its course and use <code>StateMachineBehaviours</code> for the more robust synchronization aspect to call into your code. Basically, onStateEnter, if the state.IsTag(&quot;Blocking&quot;) -&gt; call some function and onStateExit, if state.IsTag(&quot;Blocking&quot;) -&gt; call some function unblocking everything.</p>
<p>Here is a minimal working example of the Tag + SMB solution:
I set up a project, downloaded two animated 3d characters and threw them in the scene. Both have an animator with a graph much like yours (although simplified for the sake of brevity). The only reason I've set up the Dictionaries are just to make the logging more readable, otherwise I'd be showing the hashes. They are not needed.</p>
<p>Monster setup:
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/0bHbb6bC.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/0bHbb6bC.png" alt="Monster setup" /></a></p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/n2sDGHPN.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/n2sDGHPN.png" alt="States Annotation" /></a></p>
<p>Humanoid setup:
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/AcFczD8J.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/AcFczD8J.png" alt="Humanoid setup" /></a></p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/OpkeJG18.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/OpkeJG18.png" alt="States Annotation" /></a></p>
<p>The basics are that I added the <code>StateMachineBevaviour</code> script called <code>BlockingCheck</code> on the <code>Base Layer</code> so that we can track Enter/Exit events for any state and I annotated some states as <code>Blocking</code> using the State's <code>Tag</code> property. Basically we only care about the state having a specific tag, we care not about the name.</p>
<p>Each character has a script which implements the <code>IBlocking</code> interface which makes it character type agnostic. You could give me a coffee machine animator graph and provided we added the SMB on the layers and annotated the states, we could get the event notifications in some MonoBehaviour script the same way. I only named MonsterCode and PlayerCode just to give an example of integrating it in some existing scripts.</p>
<p>Code:</p>
<pre><code>internal interface IBlocking
{
    public bool UnblockOnTransition { get; }
    public int TargetTagHash { get; }
    public void BlockStuff(int name_hash);
    public void UnblockStuff(int name_hash);
}

public class MonsterCode : MonoBehaviour, IBlocking
{
    public bool UnblockOnTransition { get =&gt; true; }
    public int TargetTagHash { get =&gt; Animator.StringToHash(&quot;Blocking&quot;); }

    public readonly Dictionary&lt;int, string&gt; States = new Dictionary&lt;int, string&gt;()
    {
        { Animator.StringToHash(&quot;Monster13_Idle&quot;) , &quot;Monster13_Idle&quot;},
        { Animator.StringToHash(&quot;Monster13_Walk&quot;) , &quot;Monster13_Walk&quot;}
    };

    public void BlockStuff(int name_hash)
    {
        Debug.Log($&quot;Blocking {gameObject.name} stuff when entering 
        {States[name_hash]} state.&quot;);
    }

    public void UnblockStuff(int name_hash)
    {
        Debug.Log($&quot;Unblocking {gameObject.name} stuff when exiting 
        {States[name_hash]} state.&quot;);
    }
}

public class PlayerCode : MonoBehaviour, IBlocking
{
    public readonly Dictionary&lt;int, string&gt; States = new Dictionary&lt;int, string&gt;()
    {
        { Animator.StringToHash(&quot;Idle&quot;) , &quot;Idle&quot;},
        { Animator.StringToHash(&quot;SwordAttack&quot;) , &quot;SwordAttack&quot;},
        { Animator.StringToHash(&quot;Jump02&quot;) , &quot;Jump02&quot;},
        { Animator.StringToHash(&quot;Death&quot;) , &quot;Death&quot;}
    };

    public bool UnblockOnTransition { get =&gt; true; }
    public int TargetTagHash { get =&gt; Animator.StringToHash(&quot;Blocking&quot;); }

    public void BlockStuff(int name_hash)
    {
        Debug.Log($&quot;Blocking {gameObject.name} stuff when entering 
        {States[name_hash]} state.&quot;);
    }

    public void UnblockStuff(int name_hash)
    {
        Debug.Log($&quot;Unblocking {gameObject.name} stuff when exiting 
        {States[name_hash]} state.&quot;);
    }
}

public class BlockingCheck : StateMachineBehaviour
{
    IBlocking blockingScript = null;
    bool blocking = false;

    // OnStateEnter is called when a transition starts and the state machine 
    // starts to evaluate this state
    override public void OnStateEnter(Animator animator, 
    AnimatorStateInfo stateInfo, int layerIndex)
    {
        if(blockingScript == null)
        {
            blockingScript = animator.gameObject.GetComponent&lt;IBlocking&gt;();
        }

        if (blockingScript != null)
        {
            if (blocking
            &amp;&amp; stateInfo.tagHash != blockingScript.TargetTagHash)
            {
                Debug.Log(&quot;We are transitioning away from a blocking state.&quot;);
                if (blockingScript.UnblockOnTransition)
                {
                    blockingScript.UnblockStuff(stateInfo.shortNameHash);
                    blocking = false;
                }
            }

            if (stateInfo.tagHash == blockingScript.TargetTagHash)
            {
                blockingScript.BlockStuff(stateInfo.shortNameHash);
                blocking = true;
            }
        }        
    }

    // OnStateExit is called when a transition ends and the state machine 
    //finishes evaluating this state
    override public void OnStateExit(Animator animator, 
    AnimatorStateInfo stateInfo, int layerIndex)
    {
        if (blockingScript != null
            &amp;&amp; stateInfo.tagHash == blockingScript.TargetTagHash)
        {
            blockingScript.UnblockStuff(stateInfo.shortNameHash);
            blocking = false;
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>In the end we get the following:
<a href="https://i.sstatic.net/AJF2p4B8.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/AJF2p4B8.png" alt="Blocking states reported" /></a></p>
<p>You can see here that as soon as the SwordAttack on the female fighter is entered, the SMB StateEnter reports it to the PlayerCode script, even as we are transitioning with a blend to the state. If we had set the transition to fully player the Idle before transitioning away or making the transition instant, the effect would have been more obvious.</p>
<p>The scorpion monster has shorter animations so you can see that it's already entered and exited the blocking state. Not only that, but it has detected when the state has been entered again while transitiniong away from the Blocking state into another.</p>
<p>You can download a working minimal project, you can play with, <a href="https://github.com/NPatch/AnimationBehaviourTest" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.</p>
